-- gap[104] · a TRUE constant with an `else`. Under the defect NEITHER arm ran,
-- which no mis-taken-branch theory can explain and which is why the two-armed
-- shape is the sharpest form of the bug.
--
-- The trailing `print("after")` is load-bearing: it pushes the `if/else` out of
-- the function's tail slot, so both arms lower with `allow_return = false` and
-- take the path that was broken. Without it the arms ARE the answer, go through
-- the return path, and print correctly even on an unfixed compiler — the
-- fixture would have been green against the bug it exists to catch.

main = (): i64
    if 42 > 10
        print("then")
        print("thenlast")
    else
        print("else")
        print("elselast")
    print("after")
    0
